{
  "id": "5z77qh5",
  "title": "SECTION 4: HISTORY OF KARNATAKA",
  "order": 4,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "xxt71rd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.1 Pre-Historic & Early History"
    },
    {
      "id": "w276hh7",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Prehistoric Sites</strong></p>",
            "<p>Hunsgi Valley (Yadgir) — lower Paleolithic tools (Acheulean); Brahmagiri (Chitradurga) — Neolithic-Megalithic; Tekkalakota, Maski</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Rock Edicts of Ashoka</strong></p>",
            "<p>Brahmagiri (Chitradurga), Maski (Raichur), Gavimath, Palkigundu, Siddapura — Karnataka has most Ashokan edicts of any state</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Maski Edict</strong></p>",
            "<p>First edict to name 'Ashoka' — resolved identification of 'Devanampiya Piyadasi' as Ashoka (discovered 1915)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Satavahana Influence</strong></p>",
            "<p>Ruled Deccan including parts of Karnataka (1st century BCE – 3rd century CE); Amaravati style sculpture</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Kadambas (345–525 CE)</strong></p>",
            "<p>First independent native dynasty in Karnataka; capital Banavasi (Uttara Kannada); Mayurasharma was founder; Kannada language inscriptions begin</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qjmum4l",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.2 Ancient Dynasties — Chronological"
    },
    {
      "id": "h3lt2tv",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Dynasty</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Period</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Capital</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Rulers</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Major Contributions</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kadambas</p>",
            "<p>345–525 CE</p>",
            "<p>Banavasi</p>",
            "<p>Mayurasharma, Kakusthavarma</p>",
            "<p>First to use Kannada in royal inscriptions; Halmidir copper plate inscription</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Western Gangas</p>",
            "<p>350–1000 CE</p>",
            "<p>Talakad (near Mysuru)</p>",
            "<p>Shivamara II, Rachamalla IV</p>",
            "<p>Jain patrons; Gomateshwara statue (981 CE) at Shravanabelagola; earliest Kannada literature</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kadambas of Goa</p>",
            "<p>960–1300 CE</p>",
            "<p>Chandrapur (Goa)</p>",
            "<p>Shasthadeva</p>",
            "<p>Branch of Kadambas; ruled coastal Karnataka &amp; Goa</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chalukyas of Badami (Vatapi)</p>",
            "<p>535–757 CE</p>",
            "<p>Vatapi (Badami)</p>",
            "<p>Pulakesi I, Pulakesi II, Vikramaditya I</p>",
            "<p>Pulakesi II defeated Harsha; cave temples at Badami, Aihole, Pattadakal; early Dravidian architecture</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rashtrakutas</p>",
            "<p>753–982 CE</p>",
            "<p>Manyakheta (Malkhed)</p>",
            "<p>Dantidurga, Amoghavarsha I, Krishna I</p>",
            "<p>Kailasa Temple at Ellora (Krishna I); Amoghavarsha — patron of Kannada literature; Kavirajamarga (oldest Kannada literary work)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Western Chalukyas of Kalyani</p>",
            "<p>973–1200 CE</p>",
            "<p>Kalyani (Basavakalyan)</p>",
            "<p>Tailapa II, Vikramaditya VI, Someshvara I</p>",
            "<p>Vikramaditya VI — era named Vikrama Chalukya Shaka; patronised Kannada &amp; Sanskrit; Basavanna's Vachana movement arose in their era</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hoysalas</p>",
            "<p>1000–1346 CE</p>",
            "<p>Dwarasamudra (Halebidu)</p>",
            "<p>Vishnuvardhana, Veeraballala II, Veeraballala III</p>",
            "<p>Star-shaped temple architecture at Belur &amp; Halebidu; intricately carved soapstone; Vishnuvardhana converted from Jainism (Ramanuja's influence); Belur = Chennakeshava Temple</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kalachuris of Kalyani</p>",
            "<p>1130–1184 CE</p>",
            "<p>Kalyani</p>",
            "<p>Bijjala II</p>",
            "<p>Basavanna was minister; Vachana movement / Lingayat reform; Basavanna dismissed after Bijjala's assassination</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Vijayanagara Empire</p>",
            "<p>1336–1646 CE</p>",
            "<p>Hampi (Vijayanagara)</p>",
            "<p>Harihara I, Bukka I, Krishnadevaraya, Achyuta Raya</p>",
            "<p>Founded by Harihara &amp; Bukka (Kampili kingdom remnants advised by Vidyaranya); Krishnadevaraya greatest ruler; Ashtadiggajas (8 literary giants); Brindavana of Vyasatirtha; trade flourished</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bahmani Sultanate</p>",
            "<p>1347–1527 CE</p>",
            "<p>Gulbarga → Bidar</p>",
            "<p>Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah (founder)</p>",
            "<p>Split into 5 Deccan Sultanates: Bijapur, Golconda, Ahmednagar, Bidar, Berar</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bijapur (Adil Shahi)</p>",
            "<p>1490–1686 CE</p>",
            "<p>Bijapur (Vijayapura)</p>",
            "<p>Yusuf Adil Shah, Ibrahim Adil Shah II, Ali Adil Shah II</p>",
            "<p>Gol Gumbaz (Ali Adil Shah II's tomb); Ibrahim Rauza; Battle of Talikota (1565) with Vijayanagara</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bidar Sultanate (Baridshahi)</p>",
            "<p>1492–1619 CE</p>",
            "<p>Bidar</p>",
            "<p>Qasim Barid</p>",
            "<p>Bidriware craft developed; Barid Shahi tombs</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mysore Kingdom (Wadiyars)</p>",
            "<p>1399–1947 CE</p>",
            "<p>Mysuru</p>",
            "<p>Raja Wadiyar I, Krishnaraja Wadiyar III, Jayachamarajendra Wadiyar</p>",
            "<p>One of longest-ruling dynasties; Dasara tradition; Mysore became model state under Dewans</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hyder Ali &amp; Tipu Sultan</p>",
            "<p>1761–1799 CE</p>",
            "<p>Seringapatnam (Srirangapatna)</p>",
            "<p>Hyder Ali, Tipu Sultan</p>",
            "<p>4 Anglo-Mysore Wars; rockets (Mysorean rocket technology); Tipu's Bagh-e-Aman garden; killed in 4th war 1799</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b7gbnbn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.3 The Vijayanagara Empire — Expanded"
    },
    {
      "id": "ibjclsx",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Aspect</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Details</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Significance</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Founding</p>",
            "<p>1336 CE by Harihara I &amp; Bukka I at Hampi; inspired by sage Vidyaranya of Sringeri Math</p>",
            "<p>Resisted Muslim Sultanate expansion into South India</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capitals</p>",
            "<p>Hampi (main), also used Penugonda &amp; Chandragiri later</p>",
            "<p>Hampi — UNESCO World Heritage Site</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Four Dynasties</p>",
            "<p>Sangama (1336–1485), Saluva (1485–1505), Tuluva (1505–1570), Aravidu (1570–1646)</p>",
            "<p>Krishnadevaraya belonged to Tuluva dynasty</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Krishnadevaraya (1509–29)</p>",
            "<p>Greatest ruler; Amuktamalyada (Telugu); Patron of Ashtadiggajas; conquered Raichur; Hampi's Vittala Temple</p>",
            "<p>Golden age of Vijayanagara; Telugu &amp; Kannada literature flourished</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ashtadiggajas</p>",
            "<p>8 Telugu literary poets in Krishnadevaraya's court: Allasani Peddana, Nandi Timmana, etc.</p>",
            "<p>Golden age of Telugu literature</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Economy</p>",
            "<p>Major trade hub; Arab, Portuguese, Chinese traders visited; diamond &amp; pepper exports</p>",
            "<p>Foreign accounts by Nuniz, Paes describe prosperity</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Battle of Talikota</p>",
            "<p>Jan 26, 1565; Vijayanagara vs. combined Deccan Sultanates; Aliya Rama Raya killed; capital sacked</p>",
            "<p>End of Vijayanagara as dominant power</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Religion</p>",
            "<p>Mainly Shaiva, Vaishnava, Jain religions patronised; tolerant of Islam</p>",
            "<p>Temples, gopurams, chariot streets = Vijayanagara style</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ca2xiut",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.4 Mysore Kingdom — Model State Period"
    },
    {
      "id": "qvyssca",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Diwan P. N. Krishnamurthy (1881–83)</strong></p>",
            "<p>First Dewan of reformed Mysore; initiated systematic administration</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>K. Seshadri Iyer (1883–1900)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Built Bengaluru infrastructure; Krishna Raja Sagara planning began</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>P. N. Rangacharlu</strong></p>",
            "<p>First Mysore Legislative Council (1881); progressive governance</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Sir M. Visvesvaraya (Dewan, 1912–18)</strong></p>",
            "<p>KRS Dam; industries; economic planning; Mysore University; KVJNL; Bharat Ratna 1955; born Sept 15 = National Engineers Day</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Sir Mirza Ismail (Dewan, 1926–41)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Developed Brindavan Gardens at KRS; Mysore Dasara modernised; attracted industries</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Srikantadatta Narasimharaja Wadiyar</strong></p>",
            "<p>Last king (titular); Rajyotsava Award distribution; palace open to public</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Krishnaraja Wadiyar IV (1902–40)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Under him Mysore became 'best-administered state in Asia' (Gandhi's tribute)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Integration with India (1947)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Jayachamarajendra Wadiyar signed instrument of accession; S. Nijalingappa became first CM of Mysore State (1956)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tve0dgg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.5 Hyder Ali & Tipu Sultan"
    },
    {
      "id": "c9v0q8d",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Event</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Year</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Facts</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hyder Ali's Rise</p>",
            "<p>1761</p>",
            "<p>Took control of Mysore; military genius; French alliance; Fateh Ali Hyder</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>1st Anglo-Mysore War</p>",
            "<p>1767–69</p>",
            "<p>Hyder Ali vs. British &amp; Hyderabad Nizam; Treaty of Madras (status quo)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>2nd Anglo-Mysore War</p>",
            "<p>1780–84</p>",
            "<p>Hyder Ali dies (1782); Tipu continues; Treaty of Mangalore — Tipu's greatest diplomatic victory</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>3rd Anglo-Mysore War</p>",
            "<p>1790–92</p>",
            "<p>Tipu vs. Cornwallis; Treaty of Seringapatam — Tipu gives up half his kingdom &amp; two sons as hostages</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>4th Anglo-Mysore War</p>",
            "<p>1799</p>",
            "<p>Tipu killed on May 4, 1799 defending Srirangapatna; British restore Wadiyar dynasty</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tipu's Innovations</p>",
            "<p>—</p>",
            "<p>Tipu was first to use iron-cased Mysorean rockets in warfare; built naval fleet; introduced new calendar, new currency, silk weaving</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tipu's Titles</p>",
            "<p>—</p>",
            "<p>Called himself 'Sarkar-i-Khudadad'; 'Tiger of Mysore'; Sher-e-Mysore</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shrirangapatna Significance</p>",
            "<p>—</p>",
            "<p>Tipu's capital on an island in Cauvery; Ranganathaswamy Temple, Gumbaz (Tipu's tomb), Tipu's Summer Palace here</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "enriched": false
}